13:33:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually the most testable claim you've made yet, because "what shows up in news coverage" is measurable, and it's worth separating two different questions: (1) does honor violence/forced marriage/etc. actually correlate specifically with Islam, or (2) does media coverage make it *seem* like it does regardless of the actual pattern. **On the actual pattern — this is less Islam-specific than the news coverage suggests:** Honor killings are heavily documented in Hindu and Sikh communities in India (particularly Punjab and Haryana, driven by caste/khap panchayat structures, not Islamic law at all), and India has more reported honor killings annually than most Muslim-majority countries. They occur in Christian communities in parts of Latin America and historically in Mediterranean Europe — Italy and Greece had explicit legal leniency for "honor" killings ("delitto d'onore") on the books until 1981 and 2018 respectively. UN and academic research (e.g., work by Aisha Gill, and UN Women reports) consistently frame honor violence as tied to **specific regional/tribal patriarchal cultures** — South Asia, the Levant, parts of the Mediterranean — that cut across religious lines, not as a distinctly Islamic practice. Pakistan and India, right next to each other, have comparable rates despite one being majority Muslim and one majority Hindu — that's a strong signal the driver is regional/tribal culture, not the religion. **On why news coverage still feels overwhelmingly Islam-coded — this is a real, well-documented media phenomenon, not a dodge:** Media studies research (e.g., work analyzing US/UK terrorism and crime coverage) consistently finds that stories are far more likely to explicitly name "Muslim" or "Islamic" as a descriptor when the perpetrator is Muslim than equivalent stories name "Christian" or "Hindu" for equivalent acts by non-Muslims. A 2018 University of Alabama study found Muslim perpetrators of violence in the US received on