hat now sounds like it settled something when it hasn't. So directly: no, I don't think "every practicing Muslim is oppressed by definition" survives, because the definition you're using would also make every taxpayer, employee, and driver "oppressed by definition," which empties the word of the meaning you actually want it to carry. If you want to say something true and forceful, it has to be the narrower claim — *this specific restriction is unjust/disproportionate/coerced* — which is exactly the claim-by-claim territory we've already been in, not something a broader definition can shortcut.
