orm elements in Muhammad's life and the aspects that trouble modern standards—while acknowledging the genuine scholarly disagreement on all sides.
13:39:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is worth taking seriously as a different method, not deflecting to "it's complicated" again. So let's actually try it your way and see what it produces. **On the founder:** Muhammad's biography (via the earliest sira literature, Ibn Ishaq/Ibn Hisham, and hadith) contains both things that read as reform relative to his context and things that read as troubling by modern standards, and an honest look has to hold both: *Reform-coded:* He banned female infanticide (practiced in pre-Islamic Arabia), gave women a fixed, legally protected inheritance share where they'd had none, made mahr (dower) payable directly to the bride rather than her family, required a woman's consent for marriage to be valid, permitted women to initiate divorce (khula), limited existing unlimited polygamy to four wives with an equal-treatment requirement. His first wife, Khadija, was a wealthy businesswoman who employed him and proposed to him; he remained monogamous with her for roughly 25 years until her death, his only marriage in his younger adulthood. *Troubling by modern standards:* Multiple marriages later in life, marriage to Aisha at an age modern standards would call child marriage (traditional sources say 6, consummated at 9, though the exact ages are disputed among historians), the treatment of the Banu Qurayza after the Battle of the Trench, and hadith attributing statements about women's intellect/religion being "deficient" (a hadith in Sahih Bukhari) that most modern Muslim scholars now contextualize or contest rather than apply literally. **Here's the problem with "just look at the founder and texts" as a simpler method:** it isn't actually simpler — it just relocates the same interpretive fight you were trying to avoid. Which hadith are reliable? Muslim scholars themselves developed an entire discipline (isnad criticism, hadith grading — sahih/hasan/da'if/mawdu') precisely because early Islamic scholars recognized huge numbers of hadith were fabricated or unreliable, and they disagree with each other about specific ones to this day. Which parts of the Quran are read literally versus historically bounded? Scholars disagree, and always have — that's what tafsir (exegesis) as a discipline is. Even "just reading the text" requires you to adjudicate translation choices, historical context, abrogation (naskh, where later verses are held to modify earlier ones), and authenticity — all the same type of interpretive labor as the "statistical controls," just done on documents instead of countries. **And applying your standard consistently produces uncomfortable results elsewhere too:** Moses's law (Torah) includes chattel slavery, concubinage from war captives, and a rapist being required to marry his victim (Deut. 22:28-29) under specific conditions. The Buddha initially refused to ordain women, then relented but imposed eight additional subordinating rules on nuns not applied to monks. Paul's epistles (core Christian text, not a later corruption) instruct women to be silent in church and submit to husbands. If "founder's actions + core texts, read plainly" is your standard, it delivers a harsh verdict on most major religions' founding periods by modern values, not uniquely on Islam. **Straight answer:** this method isn't actually simpler, it just moves the same fight to a different battlefield — and even fully applied, it gets you to "the founding period contains both reform-relative-to-context and elements indefensible by modern standards," which is a real, substantive, non-hedged conclusion I'll say plainly. It doesn't get you cleanly to "therefore every practicing Muslim today is oppressed by following it," because that still requires the same leap from historical/textual fact to universal present-day lived experience that every other angle in this conversation has run into.
